'''acock''' (ạ-kok′), ''prep. phr.'' as ''adv.'' or ''a.'' [< ''a''<sup>3</sup>, on, + ''cock''<sup>2</sup>.] In a cocked manner: as, he set his hat ''acock.''

'''a-cockbill''' (ạ-kok′bil), ''prep. phr.'' as ''adv.'' or ''a.'' [< ''a''<sup>3</sup>, on, + ''cock''<sup>2</sup> (condition of being cocked or turned upward: see ''cock''<sup>2</sup>) + ''bill''<sup>2</sup>, point or end: see ''bill''<sup>2</sup>, 5.] ''Naut.'', with the ends pointing upward. {{smaller|Applied (''a'') to an anchor when it hangs down by its ring from the cathead, and (''b'') to the yards of a ship when they are tipped up at an angle with the deck.}}
